// Sigil-Logic/clafer#46: `sum` and `product` take a set of integer clafers.
// An integer operand -- arithmetic (`sum (N - 1)`), a cardinality (`sum
// (#N)`), or a literal (`sum 5`) -- has no meaning in either backend: the
// Alloy generators decomposed it as a navigation path and rendered the
// illegal join `sum temp : N.ref | temp.1` (or aborted), and chocosolver
// rejects the generated `sum(sub(N, 1))` when type-checking (`Cannot
// sum(int)`).  The resolver now declines such operands with a positioned
// semantic error (test/negative/si46-*).  This model pins the supported
// shapes: the aggregate outside the arithmetic, the navigation forms of the
// operand, and arithmetic over the set itself, which both backends already
// read as arithmetic over the set's sum (Alloy coerces an integer set with an
// implicit `sum`; chocosolver's `sub(N, 1)` evaluates to 6 here).  The
// assertions let the gated Choco leg confirm the values; the .als was
// executed with `alloy exec` when this model was added (every check UNSAT).

abstract N ->> integer
n1 : N = 5
n2 : N = 2

// the aggregate outside the arithmetic: the supported spelling
minusOne -> integer
[ minusOne = sum N - 1 ]
assert [ minusOne = 6 ]

plusOne -> integer
[ plusOne = sum N + 1 ]
assert [ plusOne = 8 ]

doubled -> integer
[ doubled = (sum N) * 2 ]
assert [ doubled = 14 ]

// the navigation forms of the operand
total -> integer
[ total = sum N.dref ]
assert [ total = 7 ]

// arithmetic over the set is arithmetic over its sum in both backends
setMinusOne -> integer
[ setMinusOne = N - 1 ]
assert [ setMinusOne = 6 ]
